{"causes":["The rise of socialist ideologies in the 19th century set the stage for the establishment of communist regimes.","The aftermath of World War I and the Russian Revolution provided the initial political context for the spread of communism.","The failures of capitalist systems during the Great Depression created fertile ground for communist movements to gain support."],"effects":["The book has influenced public perception and academic discourse regarding the moral implications of communism.","It sparked a wave of scholarship focusing on totalitarian regimes and human rights violations associated with communist governments.","The Black Book of Communism has been utilized in political discussions and educational curricula, shaping the understanding of modern political ideologies."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"communism","sourceName":"Communism","verb":"FRAMES","targetSlug":"the-black-book-of-communism","targetName":"The Black Book of Communism","context":"The Black Book of Communism frames communism as a historical force with significant human cost, prompting reevaluation of its legacy."},{"sourceSlug":"totalitarianism","sourceName":"Totalitarianism","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"the-black-book-of-communism","targetName":"The Black Book of Communism","context":"The discourse on totalitarianism has influenced the arguments presented in The Black Book of Communism since it analyzes similar regimes."},{"sourceSlug":"soviet-union","sourceName":"Soviet Union","verb":"CONTAINS","targetSlug":"the-black-book-of-communism","targetName":"The Black Book of Communism","context":"The Soviet Union is a key focus within The Black Book of Communism, detailing specific atrocities committed under Stalin's rule."},{"sourceSlug":"cambodian-genocide","sourceName":"Cambodian Genocide","verb":"CAUSES","targetSlug":"the-black-book-of-communism","targetName":"The Black Book of Communism","context":"The Cambodian Genocide is documented in The Black Book of Communism, illustrating the deadly consequences of the Khmer Rouge regime."},{"sourceSlug":"great-leap-forward","sourceName":"Great Leap Forward","verb":"OCCURS_IN","targetSlug":"the-black-book-of-communism","targetName":"The Black Book of Communism","context":"The Great Leap Forward is analyzed in The Black Book of Communism, highlighting the catastrophic policies that resulted in famine."}],"places":[{"name":"Moscow, Russia","role":"Major center of communism"},{"name":"Beijing, China","role":"Key site of communist policy implementation"},{"name":"Phnom Penh, Cambodia","role":"Location of Khmer Rouge atrocities"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: The Black Book of Communism","type":"Reference article","slug":"the-black-book-of-communism"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q334896","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Black_Book_of_Communism"],"tags":["literary work","Historical & Literary Texts","cause-and-effect"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"The Black Book of Communism contributed to the historical record of the Classical period. Key lasting impacts include: Contributed to cultural heritage and knowledge transmission. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.","summary":"The Black Book of Communism: Crimes, Terror, Repression (French: Le Livre noir du communisme: Crimes, terreur, répression) is a controversial scholarly anthology edited by the French historian Stéphane Courtois and co-authored by six other historians (Nicolas Werth, Jean-Louis Panné, Andrzej Paczkowski, Karel Bartošek, Jean-Louis Margolin, and Rémi Kauffer), published by Harvard University Press in French in 1997 and translated into many languages (English 1999). The book is an attempt to document, in systematic comparative form, the crimes and deaths attributable to communist regimes worldwide — the Soviet Union, China, Cambodia, Korea, Vietnam, Eastern Europe, Cuba, Ethiopia, and elsewhere — and arrives at a controversial aggregate estimate of approximately 94–100 million deaths attributable to communist regimes in the 20th century.

The Black Book of Communism generated an immediate and sustained controversy, both about its methods and about its political implications. The aggregate death toll estimate (94–100 million) has been contested by other historians (Robert Conquest, whose research on Soviet terror Courtois drew on, and others have given lower estimates for some regimes); the book's co-authors publicly disagreed with Courtois's framing and his political agenda (several co-authors wrote prefaces distancing themselves from Courtois's comparison of communism to Nazism in body count terms); and critics argued that the aggregation of death tolls across diverse regimes over eight decades distorts more than it illuminates.

Despite the controversy, The Black Book of Communism became one of the most widely read works on communist history and an important document in the post-Cold War reassessment of the communist legacy — particularly in Eastern Europe and France, where it provoked intense public debate about the relationship between the French Communist Party and Stalinist repression.","subjects":["History","Modern Era","Communism","Soviet History","Political History","Historiography","Cold War","20th Century"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"The Black Book of Communism reshaped the understanding of communist regimes by documenting the atrocities committed under their rule, influencing both academic research and public perception.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
